// Distributed schedule, EIGHT compute workers — the >4-worker
// robustness cell (TASK-0367). A row-band partition of the outer `i`
// axis across {w0..w7}; host runs load/save.
//
// Why this cell exists
// --------------------
// Companion to distributed3.sched.nuc. Where distributed3 probes a
// worker count BELOW 4 (and uneven), this probes a worker count
// ABOVE 4. Together they prove the partition-decompose + transfer/
// gather codegen generalises across worker counts, not just at the
// single N=4 every other multi-worker schedule used. See TASK-0367
// (cycle-213 strategic-analysis finding F1).
//
// Partition shape
// ---------------
// `loop i : partition=workers;` on the outer axis. N=16 rows, 8
// workers. base = 16/8 = 2, extras = 16 % 8 = 0 → every worker gets
// exactly 2 rows (this count divides evenly, so no spillover):
//
//     w0: i = 0..2     w1: i = 2..4     w2: i = 4..6     w3: i = 6..8
//     w4: i = 8..10    w5: i = 10..12   w6: i = 12..14   w7: i = 14..16
//
// Even division — the spillover residual is exercised by
// distributed3 (bands 6,5,5); this cell's novelty is purely the
// worker COUNT (8 > 4), proving the per-worker emit + N-way gather
// scale past 4 workers.
//
// Why this is bit-identical with naive/distributed (no new reference)
// -------------------------------------------------------------------
// As with distributed.sched.nuc and distributed3.sched.nuc: each
// output c[i][j] is the SAME sum over k regardless of which (or how
// many) workers compute row i. Workers independent on i; no halo, no
// cross-worker carry. The existing 07-matmul/reference.bin oracle
// applies verbatim — no new reference, no algorithm/kernel change.
//
// `b` (indexed [k][j]) is whole-array broadcast via the TASK-0301
// filter; `a`/`c` (indexed by i) carry the per-worker i_band slice.
// Worker-count-independent.
//
// Sync transfers only — lowers on every tier-1 backend without
// capability gaps.

schedule for "../prog.algo.nuc" {
    workers = { host, w0, w1, w2, w3, w4, w5, w6, w7 };

    place load_a on host;
    place load_b on host;
    place save_c on host;
    place madd   on { w0, w1, w2, w3, w4, w5, w6, w7 };

    // Row-band partition the outer i axis. N=16, 8 workers → 2 rows
    // each (even division, N > 4).
    loop i : partition=workers;

    transfer a : sync;
    transfer b : sync;
    transfer c : sync;
}
